+
+## Project Overview
+
+Pulseboard is a small B2B web product for team facilitators who lose proposed action items after weekly meetings. A facilitator uploads a meeting transcript, Pulseboard identifies proposed actions, and the facilitator must explicitly confirm an owner and due date for each item before it can enter reminder scheduling. The pilot is for five teams and is intended to make follow-through visible and accountable without becoming a meeting recorder, calendar replacement, or autonomous message sender.
+
+## Project Type & Scale
+
+**Type**: Web application (browser-based, responsive)  
+**Estimated Scale**: Level 2 — a focused pilot product with one to two future epics expected after clarification.  
+**Primary Domain**: Enterprise/B2B team operations and meeting follow-through.  
+**Project archetype**: `b2b_saas`  
+**Domain expertise required**: No specialized regulated-domain expertise identified. Team-workflow language and reminder practices will be validated with the five pilot teams.
+
+## Vision & Goals
+
+### Vision Statement
+
+A world where team facilitators can turn weekly-meeting discussion into owned, time-bound follow-through so that important actions do not quietly become overdue and unowned.
+
+### Primary Goals
+
+1. **Review adoption**: At least 80% of proposed action items are reviewed by a facilitator during the five-team pilot.
+   - Success looks like: most extracted proposals receive a deliberate facilitator decision rather than being abandoned after upload.
+   - Key metric: reviewed proposed items ÷ all proposed items.
+
+2. **Accountability improvement**: Reduce unowned overdue actions by 30% after four weeks of pilot use.
+   - Success looks like: compared with the pilot teams’ starting point, fewer overdue actions have no named owner.
+   - Key metric: count of overdue actions without an owner, measured at baseline and after four weeks.
+
+3. **Human confirmation before reminders**: No action reaches reminder scheduling without a facilitator-confirmed owner and due date.
+   - Success looks like: every schedulable action has both fields confirmed in the product.
+   - Key metric: number of items scheduled without a confirmed owner or due date; target 0.
+
+4. **Pilot learning**: Establish whether reminder delivery should use email or Slack without committing the product to either channel during specification.
+   - Success looks like: pilot feedback yields an evidenced delivery-channel decision.
+   - Key metric: a recorded, evidence-backed channel decision before outbound reminders are enabled.
+
