+## Target Users
+
+### Primary User Segments
+
+**Team facilitators**
+
+- Who they are: people who run weekly team meetings and are responsible for turning discussion into follow-through.
+- Current pain points: proposed actions disappear into notes; owners and deadlines are omitted or remain ambiguous.
+- Primary job: convert meeting discussion into reviewed, owned, due-dated action items.
+- Success criteria: they can quickly distinguish proposals from confirmed actions and trust that no reminder is prepared without the required accountability details.
+
+**Pilot-team members**
+
+- Who they are: people assigned follow-up work from a participating team’s weekly meetings.
+- Current pain points: they may not know an action belongs to them or when it is due until work has already slipped.
+- Primary job: receive a clear, confirmed assignment when the team chooses to schedule a reminder.
+- Success criteria: assigned work has an unambiguous owner and due date, with no surprise or unapproved attribution.
+
+### User Problems We’re Solving
+
+1. **Action loss after meetings**: discussed commitments are not reliably carried into follow-up.
+   - Current workarounds: facilitators manually reread transcripts or meeting notes and maintain separate lists.
+   - Cost of inaction: work is forgotten, duplicated, or discovered only after a deadline has passed.
+
+2. **Unowned or undated actions**: teams record an action without deciding who owns it or when it is due.
+   - Current workarounds: rely on memory, verbal agreement, or later clarification in chat.
+   - Cost of inaction: overdue actions cannot be effectively followed up because responsibility is unclear.
+
+3. **Premature automation**: a proposed action can be mistaken for a confirmed commitment.
+   - Current workarounds: manual review outside the action-tracking process.
+   - Cost of inaction: reminders can create confusion or unwanted messages based on unconfirmed meeting language.
+
