+## Success Metrics
+
+### Key Performance Indicators
+
+**Business / pilot metrics**
+
+- Pilot participation: five teams use the product for the four-week pilot.
+  - Baseline: no pilot has started.
+  - Method: count teams with uploaded weekly-meeting transcripts during the pilot.
+
+**User metrics**
+
+- Proposed-item review rate: at least 80%.
+  - Baseline: not yet measured.
+  - Method: reviewed proposed items ÷ all proposed items.
+
+- Unowned overdue-action reduction: at least 30% after four weeks.
+  - Baseline: pilot-team count captured before use begins.
+  - Method: compare baseline and four-week counts of overdue actions without an owner.
+
+**Safety / quality metric**
+
+- Invalid reminder scheduling: zero items scheduled without a facilitator-confirmed owner and due date.
+  - Baseline: not applicable before the product exists.
+  - Method: audit every scheduling event against the recorded confirmation state.
+
+**Technical metrics**
+
+- Technical performance, security, and accessibility targets are intentionally deferred to `project-context.md`; the brief provides no technical requirements from which to set defensible targets.
+
+### Project Definition of Done
+
+- [ ] The pilot workflow supports transcript upload, proposal extraction, review, and explicit owner/due-date confirmation.
+- [ ] The product prevents reminder scheduling for any action missing either confirmed accountability field.
+- [ ] The pilot metrics above can be measured from real use.
+- [ ] The pilot channel decision (email, Slack, or no outbound delivery) is made before any automated outbound reminder capability is enabled.
+
+## Business Model & Monetization
+
+### Value Proposition
+
+**What value we create**: Pulseboard reduces the loss of meeting follow-through by helping facilitators convert transcript discussion into explicitly accountable actions.
+
+**Who pays**: Not established in the brief. The immediate customer relationship is a five-team pilot, and whether the product is paid is an open commercial question.
+
+**Value exchange**: pilot teams provide use and feedback; Pulseboard provides a controlled workflow for turning meeting discussion into reviewed, owned, due-dated actions.
+
+### Revenue Model
+
+**Primary revenue stream**: Not selected. The pilot’s purpose is product validation, not commercial validation.
+
+**Pricing strategy**: Deferred until the pilot establishes sustained value and identifies the buying party.
+
+### Business Model Validation Plan
+
+**Research needed**: Yes — commercial model, willingness to pay, buyer, and pricing are unknown.
+
+**Critical assumptions to test**:
+
+1. Facilitators will consistently review extracted proposals.
+   - Validation: measure the 80% review-rate target during the pilot.
+   - Backup plan: simplify the review experience or narrow the extraction output to high-confidence proposals.
+
+2. Requiring owner and due date will reduce unowned overdue work rather than create excessive review friction.
+   - Validation: compare the baseline and four-week outcome, alongside facilitator feedback.
+   - Backup plan: revisit the accountability workflow while preserving the confirmation gate.
+
+3. A reminder channel can be chosen without creating autonomous-message risk.
+   - Validation: gather pilot-team preference and document the operational control needed before enablement.
+   - Backup plan: keep outbound reminders disabled and retain the product as an accountability-review tool.
+
+### Business Model Risks
+
+1. **Unproven willingness to pay**: the pilot may demonstrate usefulness without a buyer or viable price.
+   - Mitigation: treat the pilot as a value-validation exercise and defer commercial commitments.
+
+2. **Reminder-channel mismatch**: email and Slack may each fit some teams but not all.
+   - Mitigation: defer channel selection; do not design the product around either channel during specification.
+
