+## High-Level Scope
+
+### Core Capabilities (v1)
+
+**Transcript-to-proposal extraction**
+
+- What: accept an uploaded meeting transcript and identify proposed action items for facilitator review.
+- Why critical: the product’s value begins by reducing the manual effort of locating possible follow-up.
+- User value: facilitators start from a focused set of proposals instead of rereading all meeting material.
+
+**Facilitator review and accountability confirmation**
+
+- What: let a facilitator review proposed items and explicitly confirm the item, its owner, and its due date.
+- Why critical: this is the product’s human-control and accountability boundary.
+- User value: teams act on commitments that were deliberately confirmed rather than inferred without oversight.
+
+**Reminder-scheduling gate**
+
+- What: permit an item to enter reminder scheduling only after the facilitator has confirmed both owner and due date.
+- Why critical: it prevents incomplete or unreviewed items from generating follow-up pressure.
+- User value: reminders, if enabled later, are tied only to accountable actions.
+
+**Pilot measurement**
+
+- What: record the events needed to measure review rate and change in unowned overdue actions.
+- Why critical: the pilot has explicit success thresholds that must be observable.
+- User value: pilot teams and sponsors can judge whether the workflow improves follow-through.
+
+### Future Capabilities (v2+)
+
+**Outbound reminder delivery**
+
+- What: deliver scheduled reminders through a channel selected after pilot evidence.
+- Why deferred: the brief explicitly leaves email-versus-Slack unresolved and rules out autonomous message sending at this stage.
+- Prerequisites: a recorded channel decision, appropriate controls, and an updated product contract.
+
+**Broader meeting lifecycle management**
+
+- What: recording, calendar coordination, or comprehensive meeting administration.
+- Why deferred: these capabilities are outside the stated problem of post-meeting action accountability.
+- Prerequisites: a separate validated user problem and scope decision.
+
+### Explicit Out of Scope
+
+- Meeting recording: Pulseboard does not record meetings.
+- Calendar replacement: Pulseboard does not replace a team calendar or scheduling system.
+- Autonomous message sending: Pulseboard does not independently send messages based only on extracted transcript content.
+- Reminder channel choice: email and Slack are not selected during this project-specification phase.
+
+## Constraints & Requirements
+
+### Business Constraints
+
+- **Pilot scope**: five teams.
+- **Outcome window**: assess the overdue-action outcome after four weeks of use.
+- **Budget, resourcing, and legal/compliance**: not provided in the brief; they remain open inputs for clarification and context.
+
+### Technical Constraints
+
+- **Platform**: browser-based web product.
+- **Input boundary**: uploaded meeting transcripts are the only defined source material.
+- **Control boundary**: owner and due date must be facilitator-confirmed before reminder scheduling.
+- **Integration**: no email, Slack, calendar, or other external integration is selected.
+- **Performance, scale, security, and retention**: not specified; they require a context decision before implementation.
+
+### User Experience Constraints
+
+- The facilitator, not the extraction system, makes the final decision about whether a proposal becomes an action and who owns it.
+- The workflow must make missing owner or due-date information visible before scheduling is possible.
+- Accessibility, localization, device support, and offline behavior are not specified and will be established during context and UX work.
+
+## Project Context
+
+### Business Context
+
+Weekly team meetings create proposed actions, but existing follow-through loses them or leaves them unowned. The five-team pilot creates a bounded opportunity to test whether a review-first workflow meaningfully improves accountability before making commercial or integration commitments.
+
+### Technical Context
+
+This is a greenfield web product. No existing application, integration, or technology stack is present in the workspace. The transcript-processing mechanism and all external delivery choices remain deliberately open for later technical assessment.
+
+### Organizational Context
+
+The known stakeholders are pilot-team facilitators and their participating team members. No sponsor, technical lead, support team, or delivery ownership model is supplied; the project should not assume any of them.
+
+## Risks & Mitigation
+
+### Critical Risks
+
+1. **Low-quality action proposals**: Medium probability.
+   - Description: extraction may miss a relevant action or propose something that was not a real commitment.
+   - Impact: facilitators lose trust or spend too much time correcting output.
+   - Mitigation: keep facilitators in the confirmation loop; measure review behavior and pilot feedback.
+   - Owner: pilot facilitator, pending named project ownership.
+
+2. **Review friction prevents adoption**: Medium probability.
+   - Description: requiring owner and due date could make the workflow slower than current habits.
+   - Impact: the 80% review target is missed and actions remain unmanaged.
+   - Mitigation: use pilot review-rate data to identify friction while retaining the accountability boundary.
+   - Owner: pilot facilitator, pending named project ownership.
+
+3. **Reminder channel is unsuitable or unsafe**: Medium probability.
+   - Description: email or Slack may not fit all teams, and premature messaging could violate the non-autonomous boundary.
+   - Impact: reminder delivery creates unwanted messages or blocks pilot progress.
+   - Mitigation: defer channel selection and outbound sending until a documented decision and controls exist.
+   - Owner: product owner, pending named project ownership.
+
+4. **Success metric lacks a reliable baseline**: Medium probability.
+   - Description: pilot teams may not currently track unowned overdue actions consistently.
+   - Impact: the 30% improvement cannot be credibly assessed.
+   - Mitigation: define and capture a baseline before the four-week pilot starts.
+   - Owner: pilot facilitator, pending named project ownership.
+
+### Key Assumptions
+
+1. Uploaded transcripts contain enough information to surface useful proposed actions.
+   - Validation: compare proposed items with facilitator review decisions during the pilot.
+   - Backup plan: constrain what qualifies as a proposal or limit use to transcript formats that support reliable review.
+
+2. Facilitators can authoritatively confirm owners and due dates after the meeting.
+   - Validation: observe completion of the required fields during review.
+   - Backup plan: retain actions as unschedulable until the team resolves missing accountability details.
+
+3. Five teams can provide enough evidence for a directional pilot decision.
+   - Validation: monitor participation and the stated success metrics across the full pilot cohort.
+   - Backup plan: extend validation only after documenting why the original pilot could not answer the question.
+
+## Implementation Approach
+
+### Delivery Strategy
+
+- **Approach**: a bounded pilot, with scope constrained to the post-meeting accountability workflow.
+- **MVP definition**: upload a transcript; identify proposed actions; require facilitator review, owner, and due date; prevent reminder scheduling until those confirmations are present; measure pilot outcomes.
+- **Rollout plan**: introduce the product to five pilot teams and assess results after four weeks.
+- **Feedback loops**: review-rate telemetry, baseline-versus-four-week accountability measurement, and facilitator feedback about review and reminder-channel needs.
+
+### High-Level Phases
+
+No epic plan is created at this stage. The intended delivery sequence is: establish the accountable review workflow, run the five-team pilot, then decide whether to extend the product with a reminder delivery channel or broader capabilities.
+
